# MTR Insight — A-DSS (Airport Decision Support System)
# Repository-wide rules for Cursor. Keep concise; details live in linked docs.

## Product and boundaries
- Short name: MTR Insight. Preserve the official product name.
- Academic MIS (SIG) with a decision support component for Los Garzones
  Airport (MTR), Monteria, Colombia. Five strategic KPIs, alerts and
  explainable scenarios.
- Not an official Airplan product, live airport integration, ATC system or
  certified operational decision engine.
- Use synthetic or explicitly authorized aggregate data. Label demos,
  assumptions, forecasts and verified reference facts separately.

## Stack (fixed)
- Next.js App Router, React, TypeScript strict. PostgreSQL (Step 7 model) for
  the full SIG; versioned JSON/CSV fixtures for the base prototype. Vercel.
  Figma for specs and handoff.
- Styling: CSS Modules + global tokens only. Tailwind, CSS-in-JS, utility
  classes and UI kits (shadcn, MUI, Chakra, etc.) are banned.
- Fonts: next/font/google. Archivo (titles, wdth axis), IBM Plex Sans (text
  and UI), B612 Mono (KPIs, codes, dates) per Notion design philosophy
  (DESIGN.md §3).
- Motion: gsap >= 3.13 (ScrollTrigger, SplitText, DrawSVG are included and
  free) + @gsap/react on landing and SIG (ScrollTrigger landing only);
  motion/react for SIG layout; three for the landing scene only. Icons: lucide-react. Confirm in package.json; ask before installing.
- Landing is dark-only. SIG is dark by default with light support.
- DSS features marked as needing approval in DESIGN.md §9 require explicit
  approval before implementation.
- Chart library: pending decision (candidates: visx, ECharts). Do not add one
  without approval. ORM, identity provider and test runner: reuse repo choices.
- Preserve package manager, lockfile and versions. No silent upgrades.
- Base prototype and complete SIG are different acceptance scopes. Never
  present mock auth, placeholders or static fixtures as complete.
- Supabase Auth is a planning example, not a selected provider.

## Read first
- Inspect README, package.json, lockfile, existing code and local docs before
  editing. Discover real scripts; never assume one exists.
- Read DESIGN.md before ANY UI change. It overrides personal taste and
  generic patterns. If DESIGN.md and Figma disagree, ask.
- Project specs via Notion MCP when available:
  KPIs (Step 5): https://app.notion.com/p/a89bad1f0bce401c8912300b4fcdb7fe
  Data/ETL (Step 7): https://app.notion.com/p/52d5618d3d564a1d88fc52a2cf5c9aba
  Screens/DSS (Step 8): https://app.notion.com/p/8e31e8e826b946c38e11592377d422f1
  Prototype/data (Step 9): https://app.notion.com/p/8f2d1ccb5bdf406aa97631e4faa45eea
  Security (Step 10): https://app.notion.com/p/ca97022a8c7440389b0477df3f822a64
  Validation (Step 11): https://app.notion.com/p/2f541471f9914a3a82ecf5a892b6114b
- If unavailable, request approved local copies. Never pretend to have read them.
- Record approved decisions, source conflicts and requirement-to-test mappings
  in repo docs. Never change scope silently.

## Architecture and coding
- Separate presentation, application services, domain calculations and data
  adapters. One typed contract shared by fixture and PostgreSQL adapters.
- KPIs, alert evaluation and DSS math are pure, tested domain functions.
  Components only render results; they never redefine formulas.
- Credentials, privileged clients and protected queries stay server-side.
- Validate untrusted input at every boundary; types are not validation.
- Parameterized queries, versioned migrations, reproducible seeds, explicit
  fact grain. Prevent duplicate joins and double counting.
- Explicit units, currencies, time windows and nullability. No unsafe any,
  hidden constants, fabricated outputs or swallowed errors.
- Code and technical docs in English; UI copy in approved Spanish/English via
  the i18n dictionary. Never hardcode user-facing strings in components.
- America/Bogota for business dates. Inject an as-of date for demos; never
  compute fixtures from today.

## Required functional coverage
- P1 executive dashboard (K1-K5, trends, freshness, prioritized alerts).
- P2 routes/occupancy by route, airline, aircraft, period.
- P3 regulatory/contractual requirements, evidence, owners, deadlines.
- P4 infrastructure, capacity, peak demand, verified technical references.
- P5 route development and regional international demand capture.
- P6 2045 planning, scenario comparison, DSS simulators.
- P7 data quality, source availability, ETL status, conflicts.
- Administration is permission-controlled, not a replacement for P7.
- Apply authorized filters and export permissions consistently everywhere.

## KPI and alert invariants
- K1 = sum(boarded) / sum(offered seats), never mean of flight percentages.
  Green >=70%; yellow >=65% and <70%; red <65%.
- K2 = requirements satisfied with valid evidence / applicable requirements.
  A completed flag without evidence and approval date is not satisfied.
  Evaluate as of the selected date. Critical overdue items force red;
  quarterly compliance <90% is red. Overdue noncritical = yellow; warn at
  30, 15 and 7 days.
- K3 = rolling 12-month passengers / declared annual capacity.
  Green <80%; yellow >=80% and <90%; red >=90%. Peak-hour utilization is
  evaluated separately with compatible units.
- K4 = direct international departures from MTR / international travelers
  from the defined catchment (matching geography and windows).
  Green >=20%; yellow >=10% and <20%; red <10%.
- K5 = critical records loaded on time and validated / expected critical
  records. Green >=98%; yellow >=95% and <98%; red <95% or unresolved
  technical-data conflicts under the approved rule. Expose freshness/partial.
- Evaluate thresholds on raw values; round only for display. Missing or zero
  denominators return an explicit no-data state, never a false green.
- Version thresholds centrally. Alerts expose trigger, period, entity,
  severity and owner; reruns must not duplicate events.
- Known conflict: Step 11 CP-02 escalates PG-02 after two red weeks; Steps 5
  and 8 say three. Flag it; do not mark that case passed or rewrite the
  policy/test without an approved decision.
- Weekly fixture views are labeled demo windows, not business frequencies.

## ETL, quality and provenance
- Keep the six facts: HECHO_VUELO, HECHO_REQUISITO, HECHO_CAPACIDAD,
  HECHO_DEMANDA, HECHO_CALIDAD_DATO, HECHO_PROYECCION, with their dimensions.
- Implement RC-01..RC-08 (completeness, uniqueness, ranges, pax/seat
  consistency, timeliness, traceability, evidence, single adopted technical
  source) with the specified failure actions.
- Log source, timestamps, rule version, accepted/rejected rows and reasons.
  Quarantine invalid data; idempotent reruns; visible failures.
- Track source, verification date, dataset version and status (verified,
  pending, conflicting, simulated). Never turn a committee-adopted value into
  an official fact or overwrite disagreement without an auditable resolution.
- Reference values are configurable with provenance, never scattered in UI.
- Retention targets (raw 90 d, warehouse >=5 y, audit >=12 m) are targets;
  document deployment limits; never claim legal certification.

## DSS correctness
- Aircraft break-even, Satena/Copa demand allocation, capacity to 2045.
  Editable inputs; save/compare scenarios in the full SIG.
- ERJ-145 = 50 seats; B737 = 160 seats is a scenario assumption only.
- Keep the Step 8 airport revenue/cost break-even model. Validate currency,
  cost basis and units; ask if variable costs depend on passenger volume.
  Never swap in an airline fare model. Break-even >100% = infeasible, not capped.
- ceil() for minimum whole passengers; explicit frequencies and direction.
- P(t) = P(base) * (1 + rate)^(t - baseYear), explicit base year and
  provenance; low/base/high = 2.5% / 3.9% / 5.5%. 3.9% is a global reference,
  not a validated Monteria forecast. Show assumptions and sensitivity.
- Mark capacity triggers at 80% and 90%; report when already met.
- Runway length alone cannot certify aircraft suitability; label illustrative.
- Recommendations support human judgment; never execute operations.

## Security and privacy
- Full SIG: real authentication, MFA, server-side authorization. A role
  dropdown is a demo, not a control. Deny by default; Step 8/10 matrix.
- Protect reads, writes, exports, aggregates and scenarios, not only nav.
- Airline accounts see only their airline. IT admin does not imply access
  to protected business/border content. Enforce row rules in DB policies and
  server queries; test cross-role, cross-airline and bypass risks.
- Migration/manifest data only as aggregates by flight/time slot. Never store
  identifiable passenger or biometric data in fixtures, logs or git.
- No secrets in git; value-free .env.example; secure sessions; sanitized logs.
- Audit access, exports and admin changes. Never claim MFA, encryption,
  isolation or compliance without evidence. No live Airplan/ARINC/Biomig links.

## UI engineering (summary; DESIGN.md is authoritative)
- One Component.tsx + Component.module.css per component, co-located.
  camelCase class names; state via data-* attributes, not class soup.
- Only var(--token) values in modules. No hex, px font sizes or ad-hoc
  shadows in feature code. New token => update DESIGN.md first.
- No :global outside globals.css. No !important. Logical properties.
- Every font stack ends in sans-serif or monospace, never serif.
- Sentence case everywhere. Uppercase only for sign tags (DEMO, MTR, SKMR).
- No decorative codes (P1, K3, AL) in navigation; codes go in breadcrumbs,
  tooltips or table cells where they carry meaning.
- Status = StatusPill (glyph + label + tint). No colored left borders, no
  outlined red boxes, no color-only states.
- Numbers: tabular-nums, locale formatting via Intl, unit visually smaller.
- Animate transform/opacity only. GSAP via useGSAP with scope and cleanup;
  gsap.matchMedia for reduced motion. ScrollTrigger only on the landing.
- Count-up is allowed per DESIGN.md §6.4: final value in HTML and for
  screen readers, status shown only once the number settles, no loops.
- No purple or violet anywhere (series included); no glassmorphism outside
  the nav blur.
- Three.js: single lazy canvas (dynamic import, ssr:false), paused offscreen,
  DPR <= 1.5, static poster fallback. Never encode KPI data in 3D.
- States for every data view: loading (skeleton), empty, error, no-data,
  partial, stale, unauthorized.
- Units, period, source and last-validated timestamp on analytical views.
- WCAG 2.1 AA: keyboard, visible focus, contrast, labels, chart table
  alternatives, prefers-reduced-motion.

## UI quality gate (required before calling UI done)
- Verify rendered fonts in DevTools (Computed > Rendered Fonts): Archivo,
  IBM Plex Sans, B612 Mono. If any text renders in Times/serif, the task is
  not done.
- Check light and dark at 1440, 1024 and 390 px. No horizontal scroll.
- No mixed-language strings on a screen. No Next.js dev overlay errors.
- Compare against DESIGN.md §14 checklist and approved Figma.
- Landing: verify hero timeline, scene budgets and reduced-motion fallback
  (DESIGN.md §6.3, §7, §13).

## Verification and done
- Small scoped changes. Test domain behavior before wiring UI.
- Run real lint, typecheck, tests and build. Report exact results and
  unrun checks; never claim success without execution.
- Check K1-K5 against manual calculations and Step 9 sim-1.0 control table.
  Cover boundaries, missing sources, invalid data, evidence dates, conflict
  overrides, zero denominators, rounding and operator isolation.
- Test alert persistence, forecast baseline/trigger years, infeasible
  break-even, reproducible ETL and authn/authz including direct requests.
- Full acceptance needs functional P1-P7, DB-backed data, ETL, K1-K5,
  alerts, DSS and real role protection.
- Update setup, architecture, decisions, data assumptions, test evidence and
  deployment notes. No demo secrets in public README.
- Do not add a license without explicit approval of the rights holders.
- Before handoff: summarize changes, executed checks, gaps and risks.